# T4 — fs.watch reliability spike — captured evidence
#
# Environment
#   OS:        macOS 26.5 (Darwin 25.5.0, arm64)
#   Runtime:   Bun 1.3.11 (fs.watch backed by Node-compatible API)
#   Script:    apps/backend/bin/spike-fswatch.ts
#   Driver:    /tmp/spike-fswatch/run-patterns.sh
#   Fixture:   /tmp/spike-fswatch/watched.yaml (re-seeded per pattern)
#   Watcher:   non-recursive, fs.watch(path, listener)
#
# Each pattern runs against a fresh watcher (2.5s window). Output lines
# are line-delimited JSON emitted by the spike script.

================================================================================
PATTERN A — direct write (`echo ... > file`), 3 writes ~300 ms apart
================================================================================
{"event":"start","target":"/tmp/spike-fswatch/watched.yaml","durationMs":2500,"startedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:08.199Z","pid":12595}
{"seq":1,"ts":"2026-06-14T09:29:08.536Z","eventType":"change","filename":"watched.yaml"}
{"seq":2,"ts":"2026-06-14T09:29:08.846Z","eventType":"change","filename":"watched.yaml"}
{"seq":3,"ts":"2026-06-14T09:29:09.155Z","eventType":"change","filename":"watched.yaml"}
{"event":"stop","stoppedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:10.704Z","totalEvents":3}

OBSERVATION
  3 in-place writes → 3 `change` events. The inode is preserved, so the
  watcher keeps tracking the file across writes. No re-watch required.

================================================================================
PATTERN B — atomic write (write to temp, `mv -f temp file`), 2 swaps ~400 ms apart
================================================================================
{"event":"start","target":"/tmp/spike-fswatch/watched.yaml","durationMs":2500,"startedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:10.795Z","pid":12814}
{"seq":1,"ts":"2026-06-14T09:29:11.131Z","eventType":"rename","filename":"watched.yaml"}
{"event":"stop","stoppedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:13.298Z","totalEvents":1}

OBSERVATION
  2 atomic renames → only 1 `rename` event. The first rename replaces
  the inode; the watcher is still bound to the original inode and goes
  silent. Subsequent renames at the same path are NOT observed.
  This is the canonical failure mode the T17 reload loop must defend
  against.

================================================================================
PATTERN C — editor save (vim-style: backup ~ + temp + rename), 2 saves ~400 ms apart
================================================================================
{"event":"start","target":"/tmp/spike-fswatch/watched.yaml","durationMs":2500,"startedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:13.384Z","pid":12950}
{"seq":1,"ts":"2026-06-14T09:29:13.722Z","eventType":"rename","filename":"watched.yaml"}
{"event":"stop","stoppedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:15.888Z","totalEvents":1}

OBSERVATION
  Vim's `:w` with `writebackup` produces the same observable signature
  as Pattern B: a single `rename` event, then silence. Same root cause
  (inode swap), same mitigation (re-watch on rename).

================================================================================
PATTERN D — `touch` (mtime-only update), 3 touches ~300 ms apart
================================================================================
{"event":"start","target":"/tmp/spike-fswatch/watched.yaml","durationMs":2500,"startedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:15.972Z","pid":13234}
{"event":"stop","stoppedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:18.476Z","totalEvents":0}

OBSERVATION
  0 events. macOS `fs.watch` (FSEvents-backed) does NOT fire for pure
  metadata changes when the file's content is unchanged.

================================================================================
PATTERN D — VERIFICATION (single `touch` + explicit `touch -m -t 203012312359`, 4 s window)
================================================================================
{"event":"start","target":"/tmp/spike-fswatch/watched.yaml","durationMs":4000,"startedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:45.688Z","pid":15620}
{"event":"stop","stoppedAt":"2026-06-14T09:29:49.690Z","totalEvents":0}

OBSERVATION
  Confirmed: even with `touch -m` and a far-future explicit timestamp,
  0 events fire. This is not a timing race; macOS fs.watch is
  content-driven. T17 cannot rely on `touch` to trigger a reload.

================================================================================
RECOVERY VALIDATION — re-watch on every `rename` event, 3 atomic renames ~700 ms apart
================================================================================
{"event":"start","target":"/tmp/spike-fswatch/watched.yaml","startedAt":"2026-06-14T09:30:09.908Z"}
{"seq":1,"ts":"2026-06-14T09:30:10.353Z","eventType":"rename","filename":"watched.yaml"}
{"event":"rewatch","ts":"2026-06-14T09:30:10.405Z"}
{"seq":2,"ts":"2026-06-14T09:30:11.067Z","eventType":"rename","filename":"watched.yaml"}
{"event":"rewatch","ts":"2026-06-14T09:30:11.118Z"}
{"seq":3,"ts":"2026-06-14T09:30:11.782Z","eventType":"rename","filename":"watched.yaml"}
{"event":"rewatch","ts":"2026-06-14T09:30:11.833Z"}
{"event":"stop","totalEvents":3,"stoppedAt":"2026-06-14T09:30:13.909Z"}

OBSERVATION
  3 atomic renames → 3 `rename` events captured (vs 1 without recovery).
  Closing the watcher and re-establishing `fs.watch` on the same path
  after every `rename` restores tracking against the new inode. This
  validates step 1 of the canonical reload algorithm for T17.

================================================================================
SUMMARY MATRIX
================================================================================
Pattern                                 | Writes | Event type | Events captured | Watcher survives
----------------------------------------|--------|------------|-----------------|------------------
A. direct write (`echo > file`)         |    3   | change     |        3        | yes (same inode)
B. atomic rename (`mv -f tmp file`)     |    2   | rename     |        1        | NO  (inode swap)
C. editor save (vim writebackup)        |    2   | rename     |        1        | NO  (inode swap)
D. `touch` (mtime only)                 |    3   | (none)     |        0        | yes (no signal)
B with re-watch on rename               |    3   | rename     |        3        | yes (recovered)

CANONICAL ALGORITHM (see docs/spikes/fswatch.md for full spec)
  1. On `rename`  → close watcher, re-establish fs.watch on the same path
  2. On `change`  → read + parse the file
  3. Debounce    → coalesce events within 500 ms before processing
  4. On parse fail → log, keep last-good config, do NOT call onChange
